<p>President Trump told congressional leaders on Friday that hostilities with Iran “have terminated,” arguing that a ceasefire that began on April 7 ended the fighting that began on Feb. 28 before a War Powers Resolution deadline required congressional action. Trump wrote that there had been no exchange of fire between U.S. forces and Iran since April 7, and the administration said it did not need new authorization from Congress to maintain its Middle East posture under the 1973 law. The same day, Trump said he was “not satisfied” with Iran’s latest proposal to end the war, which Tehran sent through Pakistani intermediaries; Pakistani officials confirmed they forwarded it to Washington but declined to disclose its contents. Trump said Iran was seeking terms he “can’t agree” to, while the ceasefire continued alongside a U.S. troop presence in the region and a naval blockade affecting Iranian oil exports.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/DiGb_Ajm4sSKMIbgoGikxPSEM2rz6jEC3Nn5oPCQVaKU6qtflDWgJ7db0ALnFuhcKu7xxE5UI05wkv-fCqokUCMAdIYHyVuXGkfoOwQp29WM' alt='A May Day march and anti-war protest is staged in front of the White House as President Trump takes off aboard Marine One.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Capitol Hill pressure: Semafor reported that the letter was aimed at deflating an authorization push among lawmakers, including Republicans who had grown concerned about the conflict and were increasingly open to a debate.</li><li>Negotiations: Trump blamed Iran’s “fractured” leadership for inconsistent talks, saying the sides would get close before “a new group of people comes in”.</li><li>Force levels: CBS reported that more than 50,000 U.S. military personnel remained deployed across the Middle East despite the White House’s statement that hostilities had ended.</li></ul><blockquote>Do we want to go and just blast the hell out of them and finish them forever — or do we want to try and make a deal? I mean, those are the options - President Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Trump administration: The letter outlined “changes in the posture” of U.S. forces in the region, and Semafor reported that officials viewed it as a basis for resetting the War Powers clock if fighting resumes. (<a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/05/01/2026/trump-seeks-to-deflate-iran-authorization-push-by-telling-congress-war-is-over'>Semafor</a>)</li><li>President Trump: Trump told lawmakers that Iran remained a “significant” threat, and he told reporters he did not think a congressional authorization debate under the War Powers Resolution was constitutional. (<a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/05/01/2026/trump-seeks-to-deflate-iran-authorization-push-by-telling-congress-war-is-over'>Semafor</a>)</li><li>Senate Republican leadership: Senate Majority Leader John Thune said he did not plan to intervene or hold a vote authorizing force in Iran, saying he was listening to the White House’s case. (<a href='https://timeskuwait.com/gop-backs-trump-as-iran-war-deadline-passes-but-calls-grow-for-congressional-oversight'>Times Kuwait</a>)</li><li>Pakistan: Trump publicly praised Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif and Field Marshal Asim Munir for their role, while diplomats described the transmission of proposals as a positive step despite no immediate sign of progress. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1996841/us-still-not-satisfied-after-tehran-conveys-another-offer'>Dawn</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/us-official-says-iran-war-truce-terminated-hostilities-for-war-powers-deadline/article70927374.ece'>U.S. official says Iran war truce ‘terminated’ hostilities for war powers deadline</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/us/article/3352193/trump-not-satisfied-irans-latest-peace-proposal-talks-stall'>Trump says hostilities in Iran ‘terminated’ ahead of war powers deadline</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/white-hou